as always an element of  exaggeration  he would not have actually eaten his id  card  i honestly can't see him doing that  boris is a performer  and performers will exaggerate  and performers are more interested in  the performance  than in in beliefs  because honestly if his liberal beliefs  were so  strongly held he wouldn't be doing this  he he would not be thinking about  backs and passports which is so coercive  he wouldn't be saying to businesses and  offices  well um you don't have to wear a mask  but if you don't we will force you to  i mean he's not acting like a liberal  conservative  why isn't he because his liberal  conservatives beliefs  aren't that strong and his desire to  stay in power is stronger  of course that masked mandate is lifting  although the pm's  talk in recent days has been extremely  cautious and they seem to have done  quite a few uh u-terms at least in terms  of the rhetoric  about how they're going to treat freedom  day pacinelli you knew boris johnson  when he worked at the spectator  what was he like then um  he was a very genial person  but you would notice that he would say  different things to different people  make them different promises  um because he's a people pleaser  and that will get him into a lot of  trouble and in the end  you end up pleasing nobody and this is  what he's been like  as a politician he sees opinion polls  which  may or may not be accurate claiming that  a significant number of people  want to keep going with the restrictions  but some of these polls are dubious like  the ipsos maury because the ft  took it apart um and  and this is why he he sort of does this  um but it's not it's not a sustainable  policy  because it's not leadership um i mean  going back to  his personality at the spectator  um he did have a tendency when it came  to  politics to agree with  one person on the right and then 10  minutes later  in another room agree with one person on  the left  and that's just boris because boris  wants to be liked  but it's not a great trait in a  in a leader especially during a crisis  um are we getting to the crux of it in  the sense that boris johnson really  supports liberalism in the sense that he  likes to be able to do what he wants  and but then when it comes to something  like a national emergency where the  government has you know  huge amounts of power over our lives  he's still quite  he quite likes to be able to to do what  he wants the problem is that we all have  to follow along with it too  yes i would say that's a pretty good  summation  um he wants to do what he wants um  so he will not observe social distancing  and when we saw that at wembley we saw  that  at the g7 summit um but  he will force the rest of us to do it  um and you get a you've got to bury them  enough  situation with this government and  that that is very dangerous for a  politician that could come back to bite  him because he's now  very he's annoying his call base  immensely uh because i speak to mps who  are getting emails from their  constituents all the time  saying what's going on this is not a  conservative government  this is a kind of socialist status  government  and it's already well for boris to do  what he  wants in number 10 and go to parties  which they all do and and not wear masks  but um you cannot flaunt this as he does  in photo opportunities and then  you know expect the british people  to to mask up well  last question petronella if another  prime minister  were in his place and boris were still  writing his columns  what do you think he'd be saying about  the current restrictions and freedom day  on the 19th of july  oh yeah he'd be um he'd be saying that  the current restrictions were appalling  infringement of freedom  he'd be crazing pericles of athens he'd  be quoting benjamin franklin  um he'd be pouring scorn on  himself as it were um i mean if he was  still the old  boris um because i i think the  old boris has sort of disappeared power  has done